ng": false, "transcript": "Now if you do have some plastic, vinyl, or faux wood blinds that are extremely filthy, just caked with grease, grime, and you're not quite sure what else, what I like to do is remove the blinds, then bring them into the bathroom and fill up the bathtub with about an inch or two of water, and don't forget to add the dish soap. Then just grab a soft bristle brush and give everything a really good scrub. Now I don't use this process for everything, usually the three towel system works great, but for those blinds in areas like your kitchen, your bathroom, or even your garage that just have a ton of grime built up on them, many times you just need a little extra cleaning power so the scrub can help remove that from the surface and get them nice and clean. One thing to keep in mind when we're using the bathtub is there usually is some internal components along the top of the blinds there. Many times these can be metal. I don't recommend submerging these in the water, just hang them over the edge so they don't get wet and that way nothing gets ruined. Once you're done scrubbing, the next step is to give them a really good rinse. Remember, we don't want to leave any of that soap behind like we talked about. And then the final step is to get them dry. So just wipe them down with the towels, or if it's a nice day, you can hang them outside, but just make sure they're completely dry before you put them back in place.", "is_relevant": true, "hook_type": "Education"}, {"shortCode": "DbZFb4OvBE8", "url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/DbZFb4OvBE8/", "caption": "How to deep clean your oven", "timestamp": "2026-07-29T21:34:46.000Z", "videoDuration": 116.766, "videoPlayCount": 173536, "videoViewCount": 78072, "likesCount": 4202, "commentsCount": 91, "displayUrl": "https://scontent-gmp1-1.cdninstagram.com/v/t51.82787-15/760124354_18446754484140899_4604443050848966940_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_e35_p1080x1080_sh2.08_tt6&_nc_ht=scontent-gmp1-1.cdninstagram.com&_nc_cat=1